{=============================================================================
  Moist air in a room: humidity ratio, specific enthalpy and dew point
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0055
 -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A room of 5 m x 5 m x 3 m contains air at 25 degC with a degree of saturation
  (relative humidity) of 75 % at an ambient pressure of 100 kPa. The model
  gives the partial pressure of the air, the humidity ratio and the specific
  enthalpy of the moist air, the masses of dry air and of water vapour
  contained in the room, and the temperature below which condensation appears
  on the windows (dew point). As in the original, each quantity is obtained
  twice: with the relations of the course (approximate formula, constant heat
  capacities) and with the EES property functions.

  Inputs : Vol room volume, T dry-bulb temperature, phi relative humidity,
           p ambient pressure
  Outputs: p_air partial pressure of the dry air, w humidity ratio,
           h specific enthalpy of the moist air per kg of dry air,
           m_air and m_v masses of dry air and of water vapour,
           T_r dew-point temperature
  Units  : SI - temperatures in degC, pressures in Pa, energies in J
  Source : ULiege, course "Thermodynamique appliquee" (MECA0002), session R9,
           exercise 1 - see README.md
=============================================================================}

"Data"
fluid$ = 'AirH2O'      "moist-air fluid of the EES property functions"
Vol = 75 [m^3]         "volume of the room"
T = 25 [C]             "dry-bulb temperature of the air"
phi = 0.75             "degree of saturation = relative humidity [-]"
p = 100e3 [Pa]         "ambient pressure (100 kPa in the original)"

"!Partial pressure of the air"
"the relative humidity is the ratio of the partial pressure of the water vapour
 to the saturation pressure of the water vapour at the temperature of the
 mixture, so the partial pressure of the vapour follows directly"
p_sat = p_sat(Water,T=T)   "saturation pressure of water at the air temperature [Pa]"
p_v = phi * p_sat          "partial pressure of the water vapour [Pa]"
"the partial pressure of the air is deduced from the total pressure and the
 partial pressure of the vapour: the sum of the partial pressures equals the
 total pressure (p is given above and stays at its value)"
p = p_air + p_v            "total pressure [Pa]"

"!Humidity ratio and specific enthalpy of the air"
"the humidity ratio is computed with the approximate relation of the course
 linking it to the partial pressure of the vapour, or with the EES function
 HumRat"
w_corr = 0.622 * p_v / (p-p_v)   "humidity ratio, course formula [kg water/kg dry air]"
w_ees = HumRat(AirH2O,T=T,r=phi,P=P)  "humidity ratio, EES HumRat function [kg/kg]"
"the two approaches give the same result (as in the original)"

"the specific enthalpy is computed with the EES function enthalpy or from its
 definition"
h_a = enthalpy(fluid$,p=p,T=T,w=w_ees)  "specific enthalpy of the mixture per kg of dry air, three arguments (cf. the EES help) [J/kg dry air]"
h_air = cp_air * T   "specific enthalpy of the dry air, h = 0 at 0 degC as in the course [J/kg]"
"careful: h_air cannot be computed with the EES function enthalpy(air_ha,p=p,T=T)
 alone, because the reference used by EES for the dry air is not the reference of
 the course, which sets h = 0 at 0 degC (h_air = cp_air*T); the enthalpy is
 therefore brought back to the 0 degC reference by subtracting:"
h_air2 = enthalpy(air_ha,p=p_air,T=T) - enthalpy(air_ha,p=p_air,T=0)  "dry-air enthalpy referred to 0 degC, EES fluid air_ha [J/kg]"
h_air3 = enthalpy(air,T=T) - enthalpy(air,T=0)  "same with the EES ideal-gas fluid Air [J/kg]"
cp_air = 1005 [J/kg-K]   "specific heat capacity of the dry air (1.005 kJ/kg-K in the original)"
h_v = 2500900 + 1820*T   "specific enthalpy of the vapour, course formula (2500.9 kJ/kg and 1.82 kJ/(degC-kg) in the original) [J/kg]"
h_v2 = enthalpy(Water,T=T,p=p_v)   "specific enthalpy of the vapour, Water property function [J/kg]"
h_aa = h_air + w_corr*h_v   "specific enthalpy of the moist air, course definition [J/kg dry air]"
"the two approaches give almost the same result (as in the original)"

"!Masses of dry air and of water vapour"
v = volume(Air,T=T,p=p_air)      "specific volume of the dry air [m^3/kg]"
v3 = volume(air_ha,T=T,p=p_air)  "specific volume of the dry air, EES fluid air_ha [m^3/kg]"
m_air = Vol/v      "mass of dry air in the room [kg]"
m_air3 = Vol/v3    "mass of dry air in the room, EES fluid air_ha [kg]"
m_v = w_ees*m_air  "mass of water vapour in the room [kg]"
"using the partial pressures, see the course: p_part*v = rT, i.e. the partial
 pressure is multiplied by the specific volume Vol/m"
p_air * Vol = m_air2 * R_air * (T+273.15)   "ideal-gas form for the dry air [kg]"
p_v * Vol = m_v2 * R_v * (T+273.15)         "ideal-gas form for the vapour [kg]"
R_v = 8314 / 18        "specific gas constant of the vapour (8.314 kJ/kmol-K over 18 kg/kmol in the original; alternatively R#/molarmass(Water)) [J/kg-K]"
R_air = 287.05         "specific gas constant of the dry air (0.28705 kJ/kg-K in the original; alternatively R#/molarmass(Air)) [J/kg-K]"
"the two approaches give almost the same result (as in the original)"

"!Onset of the condensation"
"-> the dew point is computed here with the EES function DEWPOINT: below this
 temperature, condensation appears on the windows"
T_r = dewpoint(fluid$,T=T,p=p,w=w_corr)   "dew-point temperature [C]"